{
  "map": "gauntlet",
  "_untuned": "This table is byte-identical to crossroads' apart from sappers -- same counts, same spacing, every wave. That is a real defect: gauntlet's towers cover 7.9 route-cells each against crossroads' 5.7, on a 29-cell route instead of 19, so the same table produces zero leaks for nine waves. Reshaping it was tested (base multiplier plus gentler ramp) and moves when leaks start without widening the outcome distribution, so it does not fix the cliff. Left as-is so the comparison stays honest.",
  "_growth": "hpGrowth 1.09 is TRIVIAL here (0% lost, 20/20 lives, sd 0.0) and deliberately so. gauntlet's difficulty is a step, not a curve: 0% -> 95% of runs lost between growth 1.12 and 1.125. The cause is the MAP, not this table -- a fixed route means one solution means one outcome. Held clear of the edge rather than tuned into the band, because every rate that hits the band is knife-edge. See docs/reports/2026-08-07-gauntlet-cliff-balance.md.",
  "_roster": "husk enters at wave 5 and mite at wave 4. They ask opposite questions -- husk wants burst, mite wants coverage -- so a board optimised for either is punished by the other. That tension is what a monoculture of arrow towers cannot answer.",
  "appetiteGrowth": 1.09,
